Table~\ref{tab:ablation_appendix} provides the complete results corresponding to Table~\ref{tab:ablation_avg} in the main text. 
Table~\ref{tab:ablation_appendix} presents a comprehensive ablation study of the proposed Phase Rotation (PR) and Amplitude Scaling (AS) modules under the long-term forecasting setting, including variants without PR (“w/o PR”), without AS (“w/o AS”), and without both modules (“w/o PR, AS”).
The experiments are conducted on six representative datasets, including ETTm1, ETTh1, Electricity, Weather, Traffic, and Solar-Energy.
Among them, ETTm1 and ETTh1 are selected as representative subsets from the four ETT datasets, as they cover the two temporal granularities (ETTm and ETTh).
The ablation results in Table~\ref{tab:ablation_appendix} show that removing either the PR or AS module consistently degrades performance across all datasets, indicating that both components contribute positively to the model. 
Notably, removing the PR module leads to slightly larger performance drops compared to removing the AS module, suggesting that phase modeling plays a more dominant role in capturing temporal dynamics. 
When both modules are removed, the performance further deteriorates, demonstrating that PR and AS provide complementary benefits. 
Overall, these results validate the effectiveness of the proposed phase--amplitude modulation mechanism in modeling structured residual variations.

